#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# zeta

#% text
# `zeta(s)` gives the Riemann zeta function of `s`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For `s > 1`, `zeta(s)` is the sum of `1/k^s` for `k` from 1 to `inf` (DLMF 25.2.1); elsewhere it is the analytic continuation.

#% item
# `zeta` has a pole at `s` = 1; the exact 1 gives `failure(pole, zeta(1))`.

#% item
# Exact values: at positive even integers as rational multiples of powers of `pi`, and at 0 and the negative integers as rationals. The trivial zeros are at -2, -4, ....

#% item
# Other exact arguments stay unevaluated, such as `zeta(3)`. Real machine numbers and balls give numerical values.

#% item
# `zeta` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
zeta(2)
#% output
#= (1/6)*pi^2

#% cell
zeta(-1)
#% output
#= -1/12

#% text
# A machine number:

#% cell
zeta(3.0)
#% output
#= 1.2020569031595942

#% text
# 30 digits:

#% cell
n(zeta(3), 30)
#% output
#= 1.2020569031595942853997381615114500

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Even positive integers:

#% cell
zeta([2, 4, 6, 8])
#% output
#= [(1/6)*pi^2, (1/90)*pi^4, (1/945)*pi^6, (1/9450)*pi^8]

#% text
# Zero and negative integers:

#% cell
zeta(0)
#% output
#= -1/2

#% cell
zeta(-2)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
zeta(-13)
#% output
#= -1/12

#% text
# Arguments below 1 by continuation:

#% cell
zeta(0.5)
#% output
#= -1.4603545088095868

#% cell
zeta(-1.5)
#% output
#= -0.025485201889833036

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The sum of the reciprocal squares:

#% cell
sum(1/k^2, [k, 1, inf])
#% output
#= (1/6)*pi^2

#% text
# For large `s`, `zeta(s)` is close to 1:

#% cell
n(zeta(30) - 1)
#% output
#= 9.313274324196682e-10

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The pole gives a failure for an exact argument, but `nan` for a machine one:

#% cell
zeta(1)
#% output
#! failure(pole, zeta(1))

#% cell
zeta(1.0)
#% output
#= indeterminate

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sum`, `polylog`, `gamma`, `n`
